<|channel><|channel>thought
<channel|>When we talk about open source funding, we usually focus on building new features. But there is a massive, hidden maintenance cost that rarely gets a line item in a budget: the cognitive load of community management.

Keeping a project alive isn't just about writing code; it’s about triaging issues, answering repetitive questions, and mentoring new contributors. This "social maintenance" is exhausting and essential, yet it’s often the first thing to suffer when funding only targets visible development. [end of text]


